course: geography
lesson: 21
title: Climate zones and why they are where they are
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Shine a torch straight down at a table and you get a small, bright spot; tilt it and the same light spreads into a big, weak patch. Sunlight does the same on the round Earth: near the equator it falls almost straight down and heats hard, near the poles it comes in low and slanting and heats weakly.
explain: That is why the world has belts of climate: hot tropics around the middle, temperate zones with real seasons further out, and cold polar lands at the top and bottom.
explain: The belts are bent by other things too. The ocean warms slowly and cools slowly, so coasts are milder than the middle of a continent; mountains are colder the higher you climb; and the side of a mountain away from the wet wind stays dry.
explain: Earth's axis is tilted, so for half the year the north leans towards the Sun and has summer while the south has winter, and then they swap. That tilt, not the distance from the Sun, makes the seasons.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 a whole location gets one climate label, like a stamp. The real map is messier: a valley and the mountain above it, or a coast and a town 300 km inland, can have very different weather in the same province.
fact: The tropics lie between the Tropic of Cancer at about 23.4 degrees north and the Tropic of Capricorn at about 23.4 degrees south, and only between these two lines can the Sun ever stand straight overhead.
fact: Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.4 degrees, and that tilt, not the distance from the Sun, causes the seasons.
fact: Tropical rainforests grow mostly close to the equator, in the Amazon, the Congo basin and South-East Asia, where strong sun heats moist air, which rises, cools and drops heavy rain nearly every day.
fact: Many of the world's biggest hot deserts, such as the Sahara, the Arabian and the Australian deserts, lie at about 20 to 30 degrees north or south of the equator.
fact: The temperate zones lie between the tropics and the polar circles, with clear seasons that are neither very hot nor very cold for long.
fact: Inside the Arctic and Antarctic Circles, at about 66.6 degrees, there is at least one day a year when the Sun does not set and one when it does not rise.
fact: Britain is much milder in winter than Labrador in Canada, even though both lie at similar latitudes, because warm ocean water and westerly winds from the Atlantic reach Britain.
fact: Places deep inside continents have hotter summers and colder winters than coasts at the same latitude, which is why Moscow's winters are much colder than London's.
fact: Temperature usually falls by about 6.5 degrees Celsius for every 1,000 metres you climb, which is why Kilimanjaro, close to the equator, has had snow and glaciers on top, though they are shrinking fast.
fact: A Mediterranean climate has hot, dry summers and mild, wet winters, and it is found not only around the Mediterranean but also in California, central Chile, South Africa's Cape and south-western Australia, and olives, which need hot, dry summers and mild winters, grow well in it.
fact: The side of a mountain range facing away from the wet wind is often dry, which is called a rain shadow (that side is called the leeward side), and the Atacama Desert in Chile lies partly in the rain shadow of the Andes.
fact: Australian children have Christmas in summer because in December the southern hemisphere leans towards the Sun.
exercise: Between which two lines do the tropics lie? | The Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn | The Arctic Circle and the Antarctic Circle | The equator and the Arctic Circle in the north | The Tropic of Capricorn and the Antarctic Circle
exercise: What causes the seasons on Earth? | The tilt of Earth's axis | Earth coming closer to the Sun in summer | The Sun burning hotter in summer | The Moon blocking sunlight in winter
exercise: By about how many degrees is Earth's axis tilted? | About 23.4 degrees | About 45 degrees | About 5 degrees | About 66.6 degrees
exercise: Where do tropical rainforests mostly grow? | Close to the equator | Close to the poles | Along the polar circles | In the middle of the temperate zones
exercise: Which three places hold the world's biggest tropical rainforests? | The Amazon, the Congo basin and South-East Asia | The Sahara, Arabia and central Australia | Siberia, northern Canada and Scandinavia | California, Chile and the Cape of South Africa
exercise: At about what latitudes do many big hot deserts lie? | About 20 to 30 degrees north or south | Right on the equator | About 60 to 70 degrees north or south | Only at the Tropic of Cancer itself
exercise: What are summers and winters like in the temperate zones? | Clear seasons, neither very hot nor very cold for long | Hot and wet all year round, with no real winter at all | Dark and frozen for months at a time, with a short summer | The same weather every day, with no seasons
exercise: What happens inside the Arctic Circle at least one day a year? | The Sun does not set at all | The Sun is right overhead | It rains for the whole day | The Sun rises in the west
exercise: Why are Britain's winters milder than Labrador's at a similar latitude? | Warm Atlantic water and westerly winds reach Britain | Britain is much closer to the equator than Labrador | Britain lies much lower down than the hills of Labrador | Labrador gets less sunshine because of its thick forests
exercise: Why are Moscow's winters much colder than London's? | Moscow lies deep inside a continent, far from the ocean | Moscow is high up on a plateau, far above sea level | Moscow is thousands of kilometres further north than London | London has far more factories and cars that warm the air
exercise: By about how much does the temperature fall for every 1,000 metres you climb? | About 6.5 degrees Celsius | About 1 degree Celsius | About 20 degrees Celsius | It does not change at all
exercise: Why can Kilimanjaro, near the equator, have snow on top? | The air gets colder the higher you climb | The equator has a short winter each year | The top of the mountain is always in the shade of clouds | Snow is blown there by winds from the South Pole
exercise: What is a Mediterranean climate like? | Hot, dry summers and mild, wet winters | Hot and rainy all year long, like a jungle | Cold, snowy winters and cool summers | Dry all year round with almost no rain at all
exercise: Which of these places has a Mediterranean climate besides the Mediterranean itself? | California | Iceland | The Congo | Siberia
exercise: What is a rain shadow? | The dry side of a mountain, away from the wet wind | The dark patch on the ground under a big rain cloud | The wet side of a mountain, facing the sea wind | A cold wind that blows down a mountain and brings snow
exercise: Why do Australian children have Christmas in summer? | In December the southern hemisphere leans towards the Sun | In December Australia is much closer to the Sun than Britain | Australia lies right on the equator, so it is always summer | The Sun burns hotter in December all around the world
test: My mate says it's summer cos Earth is closer to the Sun. Right? | No, the tilt of the axis makes the seasons | Yes, Earth is closest to the Sun in summer | Yes, but only in the northern hemisphere | No, the Sun just shines harder in summer
test: If Earth had no tilt at all, what would happen to the seasons? | There would be almost no seasons | Every year would have two summers | Winter would last all year everywhere | The seasons would be much stronger
test: why's it always raining in the rainforest, it's not even near the sea half the time | Near the equator strong sun heats moist air, which rises and rains | Rainforest trees make the rain themselves out of nothing, every day | The rain is blown there all the way from the cold seas near the poles | The equator has the most clouds because it is the coldest part of Earth
test: Olives grow in Spain but nobody farms them in Scotland. Why? | Olives need hot, dry summers and mild winters | Scottish soil has no water for olive roots | Olives only grow near the equator, not in Europe | Scotland is far too sunny and dry for olive trees
test: Gran says Moscow and London are equally far north so they've got the same winters. Put her right. | Moscow is far from the ocean, so its winters are much colder | She's right, the same latitude always means the same weather | London is colder, because it is on an island in a windy sea | Moscow is warmer, because big Russian cities make lots of heat
test: Sam is drawing a map and puts the Sahara right on the equator. Fix it. | Big hot deserts lie at about 20 to 30 degrees, not on the equator | He is right, the hottest and driest deserts all sit on the equator | The Sahara lies at about 50 degrees north, level with southern England | The Sahara is in the temperate zone, the same as Britain and France
test: In the game a whole province is stamped "temperate". What does that hide? | Valleys, hills and coasts in one region can have quite different climates | Nothing, real climates are the same everywhere in a region | That temperate lands never have any seasons, only one long mild spring | That every temperate land in the world gets exactly the same rainfall
test: You climb 2,000 metres up a mountain and it was 20 degrees at the bottom. Roughly how warm is it at the top? | About 7 degrees | About 18 degrees | About 30 degrees | About minus 20 degrees
test: The Atacama in Chile is bone dry though the Andes beside it get snow. Why? | It lies partly in the rain shadow of the Andes | It lies right on the equator, where rain never falls | It is far too cold there for any rain to fall | The ocean next to it is warm, so it dries the air
test: What's the midnight sun then? | Inside the polar circles the Sun can stay up all night in summer | A second sun that appears near the North Pole every summer | The full Moon shining as brightly as the Sun in the far north | Sunshine at midnight that only happens on the equator in June
test: Which is true about where the Sun can stand straight overhead? | Only between the two tropics | Everywhere on Earth in summer | Only at the North and South Poles | Only in the temperate zones
exam: Identify the principal cause of the annual succession of seasons upon the Earth. | The inclination of the Earth's axis of rotation, of approximately 23.4 degrees | The variation in the distance between the Earth and the Sun | The periodic alteration in the quantity of heat emitted by the Sun | The obstruction of sunlight by the Moon in certain months of the year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a Mediterranean climate? | Summers hot and dry, winters mild and wet, found on several continents | Precipitation abundant in every month, temperatures uniformly high | Winters prolonged and severe, summers brief and cool, found chiefly inland | Precipitation negligible throughout the year, found solely around the Mediterranean
exam: It is given that two settlements lie at identical latitudes, one upon an oceanic coast and the other deep within a continent. Ascertain which statement is correct. | The inland settlement experiences hotter summers and colder winters | The coastal settlement necessarily experiences the more extreme range | Both settlements necessarily experience identical temperatures | The inland settlement is warmer than the coastal one in every season
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a rain shadow. | The leeward side of a mountain range receives markedly less precipitation | The windward side of a mountain range is invariably arid | Precipitation is distributed equally on both sides of a range | Rain shadows occur exclusively within the polar regions
